<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Cameron (missionary)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1800">1800</a>–<a href="/years/1875">1875</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Cameron was a Scottish artisan missionary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> who worked in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> from 1826 to 1835 and again from 1863 until his death. He introduced crafts such as brickmaking and soap production, and later designed churches and other stone buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a>, including the stone casing of the queen's palace, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manjakamiadana">Manjakamiadana</a>, completed in 1873.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Cameron was baptised at Little Dunkeld in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a> on 6 January 1800. His father Thomas spent his life in the service of the Laird of Murthly Castle, and the boy learned carpentry there. At 25 he was accepted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london-missionary-society">London Missionary Society</a> for its mission in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, founded in 1820, and before sailing he spent a year in Manchester helping to develop machinery to be installed at Amparibe for spinning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> yarn. He reached Madagascar in September 1826, in the reign of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radama-i">Radama I</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From his house at Ambatonakanga, where he built the first Christian temple and the first Western-style school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, he trained 600 young Malagasy in making machinery and in public works. His brick house became the model for a new style of building adopted across the Highlands. He oversaw the installation of the island's first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press, which printed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> in Malagasy, and helped with the making of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder">gunpowder</a> in Imerina and with water power drawn from Lake Anosy.</p><p>After Radama's death in 1828 Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranavalona-i">Ranavalona I</a> banned Christian preaching. According to a speech at his funeral, the mission survived until 1835 largely because Cameron answered the government's demand for useful skills with bars of white soap made from local materials. When the whole mission left in 1835 he went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, where he traded and worked as surveyor to the Corporation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a>, and in 1853 he negotiated with Ranavalona for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> to restore trade broken off after French and English attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toamasina">Toamasina</a> in 1845.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After Ranavalona's death in 1861 opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a> again, Cameron returned. On the Rova compound in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antananarivo">Antananarivo</a> he built the Manampisoa (1866) and a royal tomb (1868), and he built the stone shell of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manjakamiadana">Manjakamiadana</a>, whose towers resemble those of Murthly House. He also built the Memorial Church at Faravohitra, another at Analakely, a hospital and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/water-wheel">water wheel</a> for the munitions factory at Anosimahavelona, and was the first to survey and map Imerina. He died in 1875; his papers are held by SOAS Special Collections.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Cameron (missionary)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Antananarivo grew from a hilltop royal town into the island's capital, crowned by the Rova, whose palace of Manjakamiadana was built in timber by the Frenchman Jean Laborde and later encased in stone by the missionary architect <b>James Cameron</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/merina-kingdom">Merina Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Little Dunkeld, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=56.56,-3.58&amp;year=1848" aria-label="Little Dunkeld on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of James Cameron (missionary)"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4831 5289h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4831 5289h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=56.56,-3.58&amp;year=1848">What was here in 1848? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
